WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.460
Secondary worlds are fascinating.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.440
It's no surprise that we're obsessed with them,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.920
that we want to explore them, learn more about how they work.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.200
Take a look at this app Google Chrome made

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.080
for the release of The Hobbit films.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.040
It's a hyper-realistic rendering of Middle Earth—

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.780
far from the sketches of Tolkien himself,

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.080
farther still from the words he used to describe it.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.620
The Chrome program can be considered an act of world building,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.200
an extension of Tolkien's own world building in

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.360
"The Lord of the Rings" and, more intensely, in "The Silmarillion."

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.480
There's something so enchanting in the complexity of his vision,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.620
in its completeness, in the completeness of the languages,

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.280
in the fact that they work.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.640
It's impossible when reading Lord of the Rings not to to feel that Middle Earth is autonomous,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.900
that it exists despite your reading about it.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.200
These days we have our pick of dense secondary worlds.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.260
Fantasy literature is filled with them.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.040
Think of the Dune series, or Narnia, or the late Terry Pratchett's Discworld novels.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.000
Think of the race to fill in the histories of Star Wars in books, video games, and new films.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.840
Or take the secondary world we're most obsessed with at the moment:

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.620
the world of Westeros in George R. R. Martin's "A Song of Ice and Fire."

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.280
None of these worlds are our own, but each is internally consistent,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.540
and each has developed audiences that hunger for the author to fill in the margins:

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.900
the gaps, the geography, the backstories, the histories.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.480
And if the author won't, they'll do it themselves.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.620
Want to know more about Coruscant or Darth Sidious?

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.800
Visit Wookipedia—perhaps the most exhaustive catalogue of a secondary world ever made.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.340
Why are we so consumed by built worlds, by the act of world-building?

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.360
Is this a generous obsession, or a dangerous one?

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.540
The great science fiction and fantasy writer M. John Harrison once famously called world-building

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:00.460
"the attempt to exhaustively survey a place that isn't there."

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.960
Harrison's point is that the world building of much fantastic fiction,

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.520
which takes Tolkien's legendarium as its archetype,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:14.100
shifts the focus of literature away from the act which is most vital to its usefulness:

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.160
the act of reading.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.480
World-building in this view assumes a passive reader.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.080
It envisions a writer who photographs a secondary world—

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.320
encodes it into language—and a reader who receives this immersive experience like a film,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:35.980
blissfully, and perhaps willingly, unaware that the world he experiences is a rhetorical construct.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.460
The only problem is that all these assumptions are based on false premises.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.340
You can't encode the world in language;

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.120
Readers are never passive—they create the text as much as a writer does;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.820
and all texts are rhetorical constructs.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.320
In other words, writing is always a game.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:01.560
It's a transaction that emerges from a writer's implications and a reader's interpretive toolbox.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.180
World-building fiction seems to deny this transaction, replace it with a cleaner one,

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.500
in which the story on offer is like a clock and the writer is like the clockmaker.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.560
The problem is that this kind of fiction creates a dependency in the reader

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.220
to know more, to know everything.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.840
I feel this way all the time when I'm watching Game of Thrones.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.900
It's what sends me online looking for backstories.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.080
Perhaps this is why Harrison's own take on the fantasy genre,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.080
his Great Viroconium novels,

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.740
resist so forcefully this urge for dependency.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.780
In Viroconium, nothing is ever the same twice.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.060
From story to story, names change, histories change.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.900
Characters recur, but it's never clear if they're the same people you met before.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.000
"Like all books, Viriconium is just some words," says Harrison.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.340
"There is no place, no society, no dependable furniture to 'make real.'

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.880
You can't read it for that stuff, so you have to read it for everything else."

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.700
"Everything else."

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.720
That's the bill of goods, the ideas and insights that spring forth

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.700
from the explosive act of reading.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.520
Now, for the record, I think Tolkien did offer that bill of goods.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.160
He offered a poetic utterance, a metaphor.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.160
It was the attempt of those who came after to literalize that metaphor

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.800
and dilute its imagery by asking things like,

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.120
"But what did Sauron look like?" or

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.060
"Just how might an elf regiment organize itself?"

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.500
It's important to note that world building is not exclusive to fantastic fiction,

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.700
it's a staple of real world fiction as well,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.900
and, indeed, the real world.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.600
It's current popularity is unsurprising given our modern world,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.200
which is its own kind of fantasy.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.500
Indeed, this is what makes world building so potentially dangerous.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.800
The world building of fiction only greases the wheels for

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.500
its much more insidious and terrifying cousin:

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:02.800
political world building, the constructed world presented to us by politics, marketing, and the media.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.800
"The act of narcissistic fantasy represented by the word 'L'Oreal'

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:12.300
already exists well upstream of JK Rowling or JRR Tolkien.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.300
Neither of them is anywhere near as successful at world-building

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.100
as the geniuses who devised Coke or 'The Catholic Church."

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:26.300
I am a huge fan of Tolkien, and "Game of Thrones," and "Star Wars," but

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:32.000
I'm also wary and worried about a strain of passivity in people that

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.100
world-building fiction seems to, at best, reflect,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.500
and, at worst, encourage.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.700
Who we are is already hugely, and powerfully managed

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.600
by interested parties all around us.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.800
Maybe we ought, at least, to seek out fiction, fantastic or otherwise,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.300
that's a bit more impish and challenging,

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.600
that demands our engagement,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:01.600
that rattles our perspective and exposes the false fronts of the fantasy we were born into,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.000
the world that was built without our consent.

